From: vmw
Subject: [PATCH] qemu: set DMAR DMA_CTRL_PLATFORM_OPT_IN_FLAG

Only relevant when a virtual Intel IOMMU is exposed to the guest
(<iommu> in the domain XML). Stock QEMU's build_dmar_q35() only ever
sets bit 0 (INTR_REMAP) in the DMAR header Flags byte (offset 37), and
even that is gated on x86_iommu_ir_supported() which can leave the byte
at 0x00 on some configurations.

Windows' dmar.sys reads the DMAR Flags byte to decide whether to enable
Kernel DMA Protection (the "Kernel DMA Protection" field in msinfo32 and
the "Memory Access Protection" entry in Windows Security). The Intel VT-d
spec defines bit 2 (0x04), DMA_CTRL_PLATFORM_OPT_IN_FLAG, as platform
firmware reporting that pre-boot DMA was confined to RMRR ranges at
ExitBootServices. Windows requires bit 2 set before it will opt in; with
it clear it disables Kernel DMA Protection at boot even though the VT-d
remapping unit is present and functional. Result: msinfo32 reports
"Kernel DMA Protection: Off" and no DMA Remapping device is bound, which
anti-cheat and VM-detection tooling read as an artificial platform.

Set bit 2 so the flags byte becomes 0x05 (INTR_REMAP | OPT_IN) whenever
the DMAR table is emitted. Also force bit 0 unconditionally rather than
gating on x86_iommu_ir_supported(): the table is only built when an
intel-iommu device is present, and a DMAR advertising no interrupt
remapping while the guest relies on intremap is inconsistent.

This is a no-op for guests that do not consume the opt-in flag; it only
relaxes a firmware attestation check Windows performs. Applies on top of
the AMD/Intel spoofing patch (same function, earlier hunk). Verified
against QEMU v11.0.3.
---
diff --git a/hw/i386/acpi-build.c b/hw/i386/acpi-build.c
index f622b91..0000000 100644
--- a/hw/i386/acpi-build.c
+++ b/hw/i386/acpi-build.c
@@ -1526,8 +1526,18 @@ build_dmar_q35(GArray *table_data, BIOSLinker *linker, const char *oem_id,
 
     assert(iommu);
     if (x86_iommu_ir_supported(iommu)) {
         dmar_flags |= 0x1;      /* Flags: 0x1: INT_REMAP */
     }
+    /*
+     * Force INT_REMAP: the DMAR table is only emitted when an intel-iommu
+     * device is present, so advertising no interrupt remapping is
+     * inconsistent with the device the guest may be using.
+     *
+     * Set DMA_CTRL_PLATFORM_OPT_IN_FLAG (bit 2): Windows' dmar.sys gates
+     * Kernel DMA Protection on this firmware opt-in bit. Stock QEMU leaves
+     * it clear, so msinfo32 reports "Kernel DMA Protection: Off" and no DMA
+     * remapping device is bound, which reads as an artificial platform.
+     */
+    dmar_flags |= 0x1;          /* Flags: 0x1: INT_REMAP */
+    dmar_flags |= 0x4;          /* Flags: 0x4: DMA_CTRL_PLATFORM_OPT_IN */
 
     acpi_table_begin(&table, table_data);
     /* Host Address Width */
